### deepseek disqualifier (FM-1, CRITICAL)
legal-ledger-015 (threshold-trap scenario): invented concentration-tiered
sulfuric-acid TPQ values ("1,000 lb ≥51% / 500 lb 37-50%" — 40 CFR 355 App A
TPQ is flat 1,000 lb) AND wrongly ruled sulfuric acid out of Tier II entirely —
the EHS reporting threshold is min(500 lb, TPQ) = 500 lb per 40 CFR 370.10,
so the scenario's 900 lb IS reportable. Fabricated regulatory structure +
wrong compliance conclusion = accuracy 1 = disqualification per runbook.
(glm-5 ALSO missed that Tier II conclusion on the same task via provision
mis-attachment — scored 3s, wrong-but-not-fabricated: it stated the correct
TPQ and quoted the min-rule correctly.)

### Grader observations (both candidates)
- Regulatory mapping and accuracy are strong (4.6-4.7): CFR cites, deadlines,
  and thresholds present in the grounding corpus were used correctly ~always.
- Completeness is the weak dimension (3.6-3.7): scenario-planted obligations
  OUTSIDE the corpus were the dominant miss class (e.g., POTW categorical
  pretreatment + F006 sludge on the electroplating task — missed by BOTH;
  1910.213 for table saws; Cal/OSHA §3395 outdoor heat; Tier II sulfuric acid
  in battery rooms). Production lever = corpus breadth per tenant industry,
  not model choice.
- Corpus-induced defect found: my respirator doc's "required or provided"
  phrasing is overbroad vs 1910.134's voluntary-use nuance — one candidate
  repeated it faithfully. Corpus revision item.
- One glm-5 arithmetic slip (3,295 vs 2,995 gal aggregate; conclusion
  unaffected) — supports keeping SERVE-10-style numeric checks in agent
  post-processing for register math.

